 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity**: $O(C + V)$, where $C$ is the number of columns in the XZ footprint and $V$ is the total number of voxels in the AABB [S1, unofficial]. The column cache initialization is $O(C)$, and the voxel sampling is $O(V)$ [S1, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity**: $O(C)$ for the `colHeight` and `colRelief` caches, plus $O(1)$ for the result structure [S1, unofficial].
-- **Performance**: The method optimizes terrain noise evaluation by caching it per column, reducing the cost from $O(V)$ to $O(C)$ for the noise component [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths**: Handles heterogeneous voxel types (solid, liquid, unknown); provides detailed statistics on voxel composition; fast early exit for invalid or excessively large volumes [S1, unofficial].
-- **Limitations**: Limited to static AABBs; does not handle continuous motion (use [Sparse AABB Sweep Test](5413-sparse-aabb-sweep-test.md) for that); capped at 32,768 voxels per test [S1, unofficial].
-- **When to Use**: Static collision detection for entities in a voxel world where terrain noise is expensive to evaluate per voxel [S1, unofficial].
-- **When Not to Use**: Continuous collision detection (use [Sparse AABB Sweep Test](5413-sparse-aabb-sweep-test.md)); very large volumes exceeding 32,768 voxels; simple point-in-box checks where volume sampling is unnecessary (use [Point-in-Box Inclusion Test](1400-point-in-box-inclusion-test.md)) [G].
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(columns)$ for cache initialization + $O(voxels)$ for sampling. The method collapses terrain-noise evaluation cost from $O(voxels)$ to $O(columns)$ by caching per-column height/relief [S1, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity:** Proportional to the XZ footprint size ($spanX \times spanZ$) for the `colHeight` and `colRelief` caches.
+- **Performance:** The sample limit of 32768 voxels ensures bounded execution time, preventing performance spikes from large AABBs [S1, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Efficient noise caching; configurable blocking rules for liquids; handles sparse volumes effectively.
+- **Limitations:** Limited to AABB shapes; discrete sampling may miss thin features if not carefully tuned (though this specific test is discrete, not swept); limited support for non-blocky meshers in related systems [S3].
+- **When to use:** Discrete collision detection in voxel-based games or simulations where performance is critical and AABBs are sufficient.
+- **When not to use:** Continuous collision detection requiring swept volumes (use [Sparse AABB Sweep Test](5413-sparse-aabb-sweep-test.md)); complex mesh collisions (use Trimesh).
 
 ## Background
-The Sparse AABB Collision Test is part of the VENPOD voxel physics engine, developed by asajid2-cell [S1, unofficial]. It is designed for high-performance voxel simulation, targeting 100M+ active voxels at 60 FPS [S1, unofficial]. The method is implemented in C++ and integrated into the sandbox mode of the engine [S1, unofficial].
+The Sparse AABB Collision Test is part of the VENPOD voxel physics engine implementation [S1, unofficial]. It is designed for high-performance voxel simulation, targeting 100M+ active voxels at 60 FPS [S1, unofficial]. The method leverages precomputed column data to optimize noise evaluation, a common technique in procedural voxel terrain generation.
 
